inting to a comfortable double-digit margin.", "watch": "Any late district polling showing a single-digit race, or a sharp national swing against Democrats, would be the clearest reason to move this toward Likely D.", "confidence": "medium", "notes": ["No usable general-election polls; forecast rests on fundamentals and expert ratings.", "Incumbent Sharice Davids (D) is running."]}, "overview": "Sharice Davids enters the November general election as a clear favorite in Kansas' 3rd District, a Johnson County-based seat that leans about two points Democratic. Every major race rater, including Cook Political Report, Inside Elections, Sabato's Crystal Ball and the Economist, places the contest in the Safe or Solid Democratic column, and Polymarket prices her win probability around 94 percent.\n\nDavids has raised roughly $3.7 million and holds $1.7 million on hand, while Republican nominee Eric Jenkins reports under $19,000 raised, leaving him little visible means to introduce himself to voters. No general-election polling is available, so the outlook rests on fundamentals, ratings and the money gap rather than survey evidence. Jenkins won his primary in August, and recent headlines show Davids touting federal grants and bipartisan legislation, standard incumbent positioning. A Libertarian candidate also appears on the ballot. The forecast expects a double-digit Democratic win, with modest residual uncertainty given the absence of polling.", "history": [{"date": "2026-09-22", "p_dem": 0.9826, "margin": 15.09}, {"date": "2026-09-23", "p_dem": 0.9689, "margin": 13.5}]}